#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f110daa3-fd31-5e34-8a88-0e002c2e418a" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator is Linear"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba322277-f833-5b0f-b6ff-1b153f0ed981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329208} {:answer/id #uuid "7b08c6d1-b0ef-5251-8fcd-8130d4bd8ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b08c6d1-b0ef-5251-8fcd-8130d4bd8ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329209} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f13992-7cd8-53d4-bc5f-ebe64335c164" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d838cc-4447-5437-91aa-e0933b13b858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68d838cc-4447-5437-91aa-e0933b13b858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329211} :answer-field/id #uuid "08551bad-3dfc-5628-ac2b-6fb8af17a13a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72030db9-28d9-5270-aae9-585679ac8d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-352246" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+7}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n|  | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $8$ | $7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $-9$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+7}{x+4}=x+4-\\frac{9}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+8x+7}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+4-\\frac{9}{x+4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+4{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-9{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}+4x\\,{|}_{0}^{3}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({3}^{2}-{0}^{2})+4(3-0)-9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3+4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+4|) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+12-9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\,\\frac{7}{4}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7d71bc2-c0f0-5504-9f79-a5f5cdb8cd92" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Function Whose Denominator Contains a Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e011deb-b0b4-5fd4-8afe-862e648f4515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329222} {:answer/id #uuid "f24a8ed1-7d98-5485-a8ea-4103a4484b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e011deb-b0b4-5fd4-8afe-862e648f4515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329222} :answer-field/id #uuid "95589281-5b67-5528-afdb-baf9b389e061" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69df8fcc-df88-5ffc-a68d-6b44fd7cf15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69df8fcc-df88-5ffc-a68d-6b44fd7cf15b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329225} :answer-field/id #uuid "300819cd-134b-5280-af9f-cd770314968c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6597e0e-678e-53cf-896b-1b7f4502efe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-358987" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4x-5}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\frac{7}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we pull out the factor of $3$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4x-5}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4x-5}{3(x-2/3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4x-5}{(x-2/3)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $4$ | $-5$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |\n|  | $4$ | $-\\frac{7}{3}$ |\n\nSo, from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x-5}{(x-2/3)} & =4-\\frac{(7/3)}{(x-2/3)} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{7}{3x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4x-5}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-\\frac{7}{3x-2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}x-7{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{3x-2}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[4x-\\frac{7}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x-2|]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[4(3-1)-\\frac{7}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|7|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[8-\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{7}{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
